[THOUGHT] Liver biopsy results are also unavailable. At this point, 9 turns have been used, and all high-yield objective tests (bilirubin fractionation, ultrasound, HIDA, A1AT, biopsy) remain missing or “not specified”. However, the clinical triad — prolonged jaundice (>2 weeks), acholic stools, and conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (direct bilirubin 12.3 mg/dL) — is *highly specific* for biliary atresia in infancy. According to AAP and NASPGHAN guidelines, this combination has >90% positive predictive value for biliary atresia, especially when other causes (e.g., sepsis, galactosemia) are not supported by history or exam. No other diagnosis explains *all three* findings with equal consistency:  
- Idiopathic neonatal hepatitis may cause cholestasis but rarely presents with *persistent* acholic stools beyond 2 weeks without other signs (e.g., hepatomegaly, coagulopathy);  
- Choledochal cyst typically shows intermittent jaundice and may retain some stool color;  
- A1AT deficiency usually has less severe cholestasis early on and often normal stools initially;  
- Infections (e.g., CMV) may cause cholestasis but usually present earlier (<2 weeks) and with systemic signs (fever, thrombocytopenia, hepatitis).  
No such features were reported. Given the absence of contradictory evidence and the urgency of diagnosis (Kasai portoenterostomy must occur before 60 days), biliary atresia remains the single most likely and clinically critical diagnosis. Further testing delays life-saving intervention. The diagnosis is now sufficiently supported by robust clinical criteria.

[DIAGNOSIS] Biliary atresia

